The total electrostatic energy of the system is the sum of the interaction energy between the external charge \(q\) and the induced charges on the sphere, and the interaction energy among the induced charges themselves. From the context answers:

\[
E_{\text{el},1} = -\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2} - R^{2}}
\]
\[
E_{\text{el},2} = \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2} - R^{2}}
\]

The total electrostatic energy \(E_{\text{el, tot}}\) is:

\[
E_{\text{el, tot}} = E_{\text{el},1} + E_{\text{el},2}
= -\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2} - R^{2}} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2} - R^{2}}
= -\frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2} - R^{2}}
\]

So the final answer is \(\boxed{-\frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q^{2} R}{d^{2}-R^{2}}}\).
